narrative:
## Contracts That Chain Together

Real systems have multiple types, each with their own contracts. When functions accept multiple contract-protected types, the composed preconditions create a holistic safety guarantee:

```xiom
type Account = {
  id: Int;
  balance: Float64;
  invariant: balance >= 0.0;
}

fn Account.deposit(self: &mut Self, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  ensures: self.balance == self@pre.balance + amount;
{
  self.balance = self.balance + amount;
}

fn merge_accounts(a: &mut Account, b: Account)
  requires: b.balance >= 0.0;
  ensures: a.balance == a@pre.balance + b.balance;
{
  a.balance = a.balance + b.balance;
}
```

The `merge_accounts` function operates on two Accounts. Account `b` is passed by value (moved), so it won't be used after merging. Account `a` is borrowed mutably. The contract guarantees:

1. `b.balance >= 0` — the source account isn't in debt (redundant with the invariant, but explicit)
2. `a.balance` increases by exactly `b.balance` — no money is lost

### Composition in Action
```xiom
fn transfer(from: &mut Account, to: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  requires: amount <= from.balance;
  ensures: from.balance == from@pre.balance - amount;
  ensures: to.balance == to@pre.balance + amount;
{
  from.balance = from.balance - amount;
  to.balance = to.balance + amount;
}
```

This function composes contracts across two mutable references. Both Account invariants (balance >= 0) are preserved because `from.balance - amount >= 0` is guaranteed by the second requires.

